# Keep scope small

Do the **minimum sufficient** work. Locality never outranks completeness.

## Choose the mode

Use **Strict** automatically for explicit invocation or boundary:
only, answer first, do not continue;
read-only or audit-only;
a stated fixed write boundary;
or a stop gate, checkpoint, thin wrapper, or minimal request. Ask before
crossing it. Otherwise use **Guarded**.

## Lock the slice

Lock **Outcome**; write-only **Mutation boundary**; **Proof**; **Stop**.

Supporting work is necessary only when omission makes the deliverable incorrect
or incomplete, violates an existing contract, or invalidates required proof.
Future reuse, generalization, convenience, speculative hardening, and
compatibility outside an existing contract are new outcomes.

After each added request, relock. If work changes class, creates a subsystem,
or materially raises cost, stop; state surface and cost; ask before continuing.

## Execute directly

- Edit the canonical source and required projections. Use one writer; read-only
  investigation never expands mutation scope.
- Proceed across files, tests, or specialists when this test passes.
- A review finding does not authorize expansion. Run affected targeted tests
  during iteration; after the last material change run each mandatory gate
  once. No reviewer-on-reviewer loop without unresolved concrete risk.
- Preserve unrelated WIP; defer optional cleanup and hardening.

## Complexity tripwire

Stop when supporting work introduces a state machine or lifecycle, transaction,
journal, crash recovery, concurrency protocol, general parser, dependency,
public API, or effort comparable to the primary feature. State necessity,
surface, and cost. Proceed only after approval unless this was the original
primary outcome.

## Stop

When proof passes, report and stop. Keep installation, commit, and push
separate.

